/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  Isopropanol(1) / water(2) at 1 atm -- THREE POINTS, from Luyben (2011).

  READ THIS BEFORE USING IT AS DATA.  These three numbers are the ones the
  paper STATES while describing the thermodynamics of its flowsheet: the two
  normal boiling points and the azeotrope.  They are NOT a measured VLE
  dataset, and this file must never be cited as one.  Luyben's own model is
  UNIQUAC, fitted elsewhere; what he publishes here is that model's azeotrope
  together with two boiling points that are ordinary literature values.

  Why they are still worth comparing against, stated exactly:

    * For the PREDICTIVE route this case runs (UNIFAC from molecular groups,
      on a component estimated from structure), these three numbers were
      unseen by every model and every parameter involved.  That makes them an
      INDEPENDENT target for this case, whatever they are for Luyben.
    * The azeotrope in particular is a sharp, falsifiable claim about a curve:
      it says where the lens closes.  A model can match two boiling points and
      still put the azeotrope in the wrong place, which is precisely the
      failure mode a group-contribution method is known for on alcohol-water.

  It has THREE points.  An AAD over three points is a coarse instrument and
  the case says so; nothing here should be read as a validated agreement.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

provenance
{
    source   "W. L. Luyben, Ind. Eng. Chem. Res. 50 (2011) 1206-1218, DOI 10.1021/ie901923a";
    citation "Section on physical properties: IPA/water azeotrope 67.32 mol% IPA at 353.4 K and 1 atm; normal boiling points IPA 355.4 K, water 373 K.  The azeotrope is the paper's UNIQUAC model value, quoted by the paper as the design-relevant fact; the boiling points are literature.  NOT a measured VLE dataset -- see this file's header.";
}

columns
(
    { name x[isopropanol];  unit -; }      // liquid mole fraction IPA
    { name T_bubble;        unit K; }      // bubble temperature at 1 atm
);

data
(
    0.0000   373.0
    0.6732   353.4
    1.0000   355.4
);
